\begin{document}
\title{\textbf{Bipartite Colour Classes, and the Step an Odd Cycle Does Not Take}}
\author{C.\ L.\ Mills (Lando\,\$\otimes\$\,\$\odot\$-operator)}
\date{11 August 2026}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
If every colour class of an edge-colouring is bipartite, the classes multiply
into a proper \$2^n\$-colouring of the whole graph, so a complete graph on more
than \$2^n\$ vertices has a non-bipartite class and hence a monochromatic odd
cycle. We prove this for arbitrary \$N\$ and \$n\$ by an injectivity argument
requiring no graph theory. The odd cycle so produced need not be a triangle:
\$K_5\$ carries a \$2\$-colouring with no monochromatic triangle, both classes
\$5\$-cycles, while every \$2\$-colouring of \$K_6\$ has one. Hence the least
monochromatic odd cycle guaranteed in a \$2\$-colouring of \$K_5\$ has length
five, and \$R(3,3) = 6\$.
\end{abstract}

\section{The product colouring}

Let \$c\$ assign to each edge of the complete graph on a vertex set \$V\$ a
colour in \$\{1,\dots,n\}\$. Say the classes are \emph{bipartite} when there are
maps \$\sigma_i : V \to \{0,1\}\$, one per colour, such that no edge of colour
\$i\$ has both endpoints on the same side of \$\sigma_i\$.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:inj}
If the classes are bipartite then \$v \mapsto (\sigma_1(v),\dots,\sigma_n(v))\$
is injective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose \$u \neq v\$ agree in every coordinate. The edge \$uv\$ has some colour
\$i = c(uv)\$. Bipartiteness of class \$i\$ gives
\$\sigma_i(u) \neq \sigma_i(v)\$, contradicting agreement at coordinate \$i\$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
\label{cor:card}
If the classes are bipartite then \$|V| \le 2^n\$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
An injection into \$\{0,1\}^n\$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
On \$K_{2^n+1}\$, no \$n\$-colouring has all classes bipartite. Some class
contains an odd cycle.
\end{corollary}

\noindent The argument is entirely about a map into a finite cube. No cycle is
constructed, and none is located; what is produced is the failure of
bipartiteness, and it is the standard fact that a non-bipartite graph contains
an odd cycle that supplies the rest.

\section{The step that fails}

The conclusion is an odd cycle, not a triangle, and the gap is visible at the
first non-trivial case.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:k5}
\$K_5\$ has a \$2\$-colouring with no monochromatic triangle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On \$\mathbb{Z}/5\$ colour \$ij\$ with colour \$0\$ when \$j - i \equiv \pm 1\$
and colour \$1\$ when \$j - i \equiv \pm 2\$. The two classes are the pentagon
and the pentagram, which partition the ten edges. Each class is \$2\$-regular,
and a \$2\$-regular triangle-free graph on five vertices is a \$5\$-cycle.
Checking all \$\binom{5}{3}\$ triples confirms no triple is monochromatic.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The shortest monochromatic odd cycle guaranteed by a \$2\$-colouring of
\$K_{2^2+1} = K_5\$ has length \$5\$, not \$3\$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:k6}
Every \$2\$-colouring of \$K_6\$ contains a monochromatic triangle.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The \$2\$-colourings of \$K_6\$ correspond bijectively to the \$2^{15}\$ natural
numbers below \$32768\$ under the upper-triangle bit encoding, so testing all
of them is exhaustive. Every one contains a monochromatic triple.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
\$R(3,3) = 6\$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
\Cref{thm:k6} gives \$R(3,3) \le 6\$ and \Cref{thm:k5} gives \$R(3,3) > 5\$.
\end{proof}

\noindent This is also why \$K_5\$ escapes: \$2^2 + 1 = 5 < 6 = R(3,3)\$. The
bipartite argument reaches exactly to the odd cycle, and the Ramsey number
governs the further step to a triangle. The two thresholds are different
numbers, and for \$n \ge 2\$ they do not coincide.

\section{Position}

Bondy's bound gives \$f(n) \le 5\$ for all \$n \ge 2\$, where \$f(n)\$ is the
least length of a monochromatic odd cycle guaranteed in an \$n\$-colouring of
\$K_{2^n+1}\$. With \$f(1) = 3\$ and \$f(2) = 5\$ settled, whether \$f(n) = 5\$
for every \$n \ge 3\$ is open.

\section{Formalisation}

\Cref{thm:inj} and \Cref{cor:card} are \texttt{product\_injective} and
\texttt{card\_le\_of\_classes\_bipartite}; \Cref{thm:k5} is \texttt{col5} with
\texttt{col5\_no\_mono\_triangle} and \texttt{col5\_classes\_two\_regular};
\Cref{thm:k6} is \texttt{all\_colourings\_K6\_have\_mono\_triangle}. All are in
\texttt{Imscribing/Millennium/Erdos/MonochromaticOddCycle.lean} on the branch
\texttt{crystalline/erdos-audit-2026-08-11} of \texttt{p4rakernel}. The
\$K_6\$ sweep uses compiled evaluation and carries \texttt{ofReduceBool}; the
rest depend on \texttt{propext}, \texttt{Classical.choice} and
\texttt{Quot.sound}.

\end{document}
EOF
