premiers}, J.\ Math.\ Pures Appl.\ \textbf{17} (1852), 366--390.

\bibitem{RosserSchoenfeld} J.~B.~Rosser and L.~Schoenfeld, \emph{Approximate
formulas for some functions of prime numbers}, Illinois J.\ Math.\ \textbf{6}
(1962), 64--94.

\bibitem{Nair1982} M.~Nair, \emph{On Chebyshev-type inequalities for primes},
Amer.\ Math.\ Monthly \textbf{89} (1982), 126--129.

\bibitem{Hanson1972} D.~Hanson, \emph{On the product of the primes}, Canad.\
Math.\ Bull.\ \textbf{15} (1972), 33--37.
\end{thebibliography}

\end{document}
""")

build('binomial_row_gcd', 'The Greatest Common Divisor of a Binomial Row', r"""
\begin{abstract}
Write $h(n) = \gcd\big(\binom{n}{1}, \dots, \binom{n}{n-1}\big)$. The answer is
governed entirely by carries in base-$p$ addition: by Kummer's theorem the
exponent of $p$ in $\binom{n}{i}$ counts the carries when $i$ and $n-i$ are added
in base $p$.

From that criterion, $h(n) = p$ when $n$ is a power of the prime $p$, and
$h(n) = 1$ otherwise. The intermediate observation worth isolating is that
divisibility at a single index proves nothing: whenever $p \mid n$ the index
$i = p-1$ forces a carry out of the units place, so $p \mid \binom{n}{p-1}$
always. Only a prime dividing the \emph{whole} row contributes to the gcd.
\end{abstract}

\section{The carry criterion}

\begin{theorem}[Kummer]
\label{thm:kummer}
For a prime $p$ and $0 \le i \le n$, the exponent of $p$ in $\binom{n}{i}$ equals
the number of carries occurring when $i$ and $n-i$ are added in base $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Lucas, one step]
\label{cor:lucas}
$p \nmid \binom{n}{i}$ if and only if every base-$p$ digit of $i$ is at most the
corresponding digit of $n$.
\end{corollary}

Both criteria are statements about digits, and every result below is a statement
about digits.

\section{Prime powers}

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:pp}
If $n = p^m$ with $m \ge 1$ then $p \mid \binom{n}{i}$ for every
$1 \le i \le n-1$, so $p \mid h(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
In base $p$ the number $n = p^m$ is a single $1$ followed by $m$ zeros. For
$1 \le i \le n-1$ the numeral of $i$ is not all zeros below position $m$, so some
digit of $i$ exceeds the corresponding digit $0$ of $n$. By \Cref{cor:lucas},
$p \mid \binom{n}{i}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:ppexact}
For $n = p^m$ we have $h(n) = p$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\Cref{thm:pp} gives $p \mid h(n)$. For the reverse, the coefficients
$\binom{p^m}{1} = p^m$ and $\binom{p^m}{p^{m-1}}$ have $p$-adic valuations $m$
and $1$ respectively --- the latter because adding $p^{m-1}$ to
$p^m - p^{m-1}$ in base $p$ produces exactly one carry --- so the gcd has
$p$-adic valuation $1$. No other prime divides $\binom{p^m}{1} = p^m$.
\end{proof}

\section{One index proves nothing}

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:carry}
Let $p$ be a prime with $p \mid n$ and $p \le n$. Then
$p \mid \binom{n}{p-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $p \mid n$, the last base-$p$ digit of $n$ is $0$, while the last digit of
$i = p-1$ is $p-1 > 0$. By \Cref{cor:lucas} the digit condition fails at position
zero, so $p$ divides the coefficient. Equivalently, adding $p-1$ to $n - (p-1)$
in base $p$ carries out of the units place.
\end{proof}

So for every prime factor $p$ of $n$ there is always an index at which $p$
divides the coefficient. Exhibiting one such index says nothing about $h(n)$;
what matters is whether a single prime divides every entry of the row.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:one}
If $n$ has at least two distinct prime factors then $h(n) = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $p$ divide $n$ and write $n$ in base $p$. Since $n$ is not a power of $p$,
its numeral has a non-zero digit $d$ in some position $j$ below the leading one.
Take $i = d\,p^j$. Every digit of $i$ is at most the corresponding digit of $n$,
so by \Cref{cor:lucas} $p \nmid \binom{n}{i}$, while $1 \le i \le n-1$; hence
$p \nmid h(n)$.

The same applies to every prime dividing $n$, and a prime not dividing $n$ does
not divide $\binom{n}{1} = n$. So $h(n)$ has no prime factor.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
\[
  \boxed{\;
  h(n) \;=\;
  \begin{cases}
    p, & n = p^m,\ m \ge 1, \\
    1, & \text{otherwise.}
  \end{cases}
  \;}
\]
In particular $h$ is unbounded along the primes and equal to $1$ on a set of
density one.
\end{corollary}

\section{Position}

The behaviour along the full sequence is therefore settled: $h$ is unbounded on a
subsequence and equal to $1$ almost everywhere, so it has no limit. The related
question of Erd\H{o}s and Graham --- whether
$\gcd\big(\binom{n}{i}, \binom{n}{j}\big) > 1$ for all large $n$ and suitable
$i \neq j$ --- is not settled by the row gcd and remains open.

\section{Verification}

The prime-power divisibility, the digit criterion and the carry at index $p-1$
are formalised in \Lean{} against Mathlib, all depending on \texttt{propext},
\texttt{Classical.choice} and \texttt{Quot.sound}.

